Indexing a Data Frame
Create a data frame:
student <- data.frame(
ID = 1:5,
Name = c("Rahim", "Karim", "Hasan", "Nila", "Mina"),
Gender = c("M", "M", "M", "F", "F"),
Marks = c(75, 82, 68, 90, 85)
)
student
Select a column
student$Name
or
student[, "Name"]
Select a row
student[1, ]
Select several rows
student[1:3, ]
Select specific rows and columns
student[1:3, 2:4]
Select students with marks greater than 80
student[student$Marks > 80, ]
Select female students
student[student$Gender == "F", ]
Select female students with marks > 80

student[student$Gender == "F" & student$Marks > 80, ]
Grouping Data in R
Grouping is useful for calculating statistics separately for different groups.
Consider:
student <- data.frame(
Name = c("Rahim", "Karim", "Hasan", "Nila", "Mina", "Rita"),
Gender = c("M", "M", "M", "F", "F", "F"),
Department = c("Statistics", "Statistics", "Math",
"Statistics", "Math", "Math"),
Marks = c(75, 82, 68, 90, 85, 78)
)
student
Using tapply()
Find mean marks by gender:
tapply(student$Marks, student$Gender, mean)
Find maximum marks by gender:
tapply(student$Marks, student$Gender, max)
Find number of students by gender:
tapply(student$Marks, student$Gender, length)
Grouping Using aggregate()
Mean marks by gender:
aggregate(Marks ~ Gender, data = student, FUN = mean)
Mean marks by department:
aggregate(Marks ~ Department, data = student, FUN = mean)
Multiple statistics:
aggregate(Marks ~ Department, data = student, FUN = summary)
Grouping Using dplyr
First install/load dplyr:
install.packages("dplyr")
library(dplyr)
group_by()
student %>%
group_by(Gender)
Mean marks by gender
student %>%
group_by(Gender) %>%

summarise(
Mean_Marks = mean(Marks)
)
Several statistics
student %>%
group_by(Gender) %>%
summarise(
Number = n(),
Mean = mean(Marks),
Minimum = min(Marks),
Maximum = max(Marks)
)
Group by department
student %>%
group_by(Department) %>%
summarise(
Number = n(),
Mean_Marks = mean(Marks)
)
Group by two variables
student %>%
group_by(Department, Gender) %>%
summarise(
Number = n(),
Mean_Marks = mean(Marks)
)
Grouping + Filtering
Find groups whose mean mark is greater than 80:
student %>%
group_by(Gender) %>%
summarise(
Mean_Marks = mean(Marks)
) %>%
filter(Mean_Marks > 80)

## Factor


Creating a Factor: Use the factor() function.
gender <- factor(c("Male", "Female", "Female", "Male", "Male"))
gender
Output:
[1] Male Female Female Male Male
Levels: Female Male
Checking Whether an Object Is a Factor
is.factor(gender)
Output: TRUE
check the data type:
class(gender)
Output:
"factor"
Finding Factor Levels: Use levels().
levels(gender)
Output:
[1] "Female" "Male"
Number of levels: nlevels(gender)
Output: 2

Factor with Numerical Categories
Suppose we have education levels:
education <- factor(

c("Primary", "Secondary", "Graduate", "Graduate", "Primary")
)
education
The categories are:
Primary
Secondary
Graduate
Check them:
levels(education)
Specifying the Order of Levels: By default, R generally orders factor levels alphabetically.
You can specify the order yourself.
education <- factor(
c("Primary", "Secondary", "Graduate"),
levels = c("Primary", "Secondary", "Graduate")
)
education
This is particularly useful when categories have a meaningful order.

Ordered Factors: Some categorical variables have a natural order.
For example:
Low < Medium < High
Create an ordered factor:
risk <- factor(
c("Low", "High", "Medium", "Low"),
levels = c("Low", "Medium", "High"),
ordered = TRUE
)
risk
Check: is.ordered(risk)
Output: TRUE
Comparing Ordered Factors: Because risk is ordered, we can compare its categories.

risk[1] < risk[2]
For example:
Low < High
returns:
TRUE
This is useful for variables such as:
• Education: Primary < Secondary < Graduate
• Satisfaction: Poor < Fair < Good < Excellent
• Disease severity: Mild < Moderate < Severe
• Income group: Low < Middle < High

Factor in a Data Frame
Consider a student dataset:
student <- data.frame(
ID = 1:6,
Name = c("Rahim", "Karim", "Nila", "Sadia", "Hasan", "Rafi"),
Gender = c("Male", "Male", "Female", "Female", "Male", "Male"),
Department = c("Statistics", "Mathematics",
"Statistics", "Physics",
"Mathematics", "Statistics")
)
student
Convert categorical variables to factors:
student$Gender <- factor(student$Gender)
student$Department <- factor(student$Department)
str(student)
Creating a Factor Directly in a Data Frame
student$Gender <- factor(
student$Gender,
levels = c("Female", "Male")
)

Counting Categories: Use table():
table(student$Gender)
Example output:
Female Male
2 4
For two categorical variables:
table(student$Gender, student$Department)
This creates a contingency table.
Factor and summary(): summary(student$Gender)
This gives the frequency of each category.

Changing Factor Levels
gender <- factor(
c("M", "F", "F", "M", "M")
)
We can change the labels:
levels(gender) <- c("Female", "Male")
However, this approach depends on the existing level order, so a safer method is:
gender <- factor(
gender,
levels = c("F", "M"),
labels = c("Female", "Male")
)
labels Argument: You can directly specify labels when creating a factor.
gender <- factor(
c("M", "F", "F", "M"),
levels = c("F", "M"),
labels = c("Female", "Male")
)
gender

Converting Character to Factor
x <- c("Male", "Female", "Male")
x
Currently:
class(x)
gives:
"character"
Convert:
x <- factor(x)
class(x)
Now:
"factor"
Converting Factor to Character
gender <- factor(c("Male", "Female", "Male"))
gender_character <- as.character(gender)
gender_character
Check:
class(gender_character)
Output:
"character"

Factor to Numeric — Important
x <- factor(c("10", "20", "30", "40"))
as.numeric(x)
You may not get:
10 20 30 40
Instead, R returns the internal level codes, such as:
1 2 3 4
Correct code
as.numeric(as.character(x))
Output:

10 20 30 40
This is a very important issue when working with data.

The difference is very important when creating factors.
• levels = the original values/categories that R should recognize.
• labels = the new names you want to display/use for those levels.
Very Important: Position Matters
x <- factor(
c("1", "2", "3"),
levels = c("1", "2", "3"),
labels = c("Low", "Medium", "High")
)
x
Suppose your original data contains:
education <- c("1", "2", "3", "1", "2")
You want:
1 = Primary
2 = Secondary
3 = Higher
Use:
education <- factor(
education,
levels = c("1", "2", "3"),
labels = c("Primary", "Secondary", "Higher")
)
education


Conditional Execution
• Conditional execution is the process of executing a particular statement or block of
statements only when a specified condition is satisfied.
• In R, a condition normally produces either: TRUE or FALSE
Basic idea
Condition

┌──────┴──────┐
TRUE FALSE
| |

Execute code Do not execute
Example: age <- 20
if (age >= 18) {
print("Adult")
}
R evaluates:
age >= 18
20 >= 18 → TRUE
the output is:"Adult"
If: age <- 15
then: 15 >= 18 → FALSE

Greater Than >:
x <- 25
x > 20
Output: TRUE
Less Than <:
x <- 15
x < 20
Output: TRUE
Greater Than or Equal To >=:
marks <- 40
marks >= 40
Output: TRUE
This is useful when the boundary value is included.
For example:
if (marks >= 40) {
print("Pass")
}
A student with exactly 40 marks will pass.

Less Than or Equal To <=:
age <- 18
age <= 18
Output: TRUE
Equal To ==:
x <- 10
x == 10
Output: TRUE
Not Equal To !=:
x <- 10
x != 5
Output: TRUE
AND Operator &: Both conditions must be TRUE.
age <- 25
age >= 18 & age <= 60
Evaluation:
age >= 18 → TRUE
age <= 60 → TRUE
TRUE
Example
marks <- 75
if (marks >= 40 & marks <= 100) {
print("Valid marks")
}
OR Operator | : At least one condition must be TRUE.
age <- 65
age < 18 | age > 60
Evaluation:
age < 18 → FALSE
age > 60 → TRUE

Therefore: TRUE
Example
if (age < 18 | age > 60) {
print("Special age group")
}
NOT Operator !: The ! operator reverses a logical value.
x <- 10
x > 5
gives: TRUE
But: !(x > 5)
gives: FALSE
if Statement: The if statement is used when a block of code should be executed only if a
condition is TRUE.
Syntax:
if (condition) {
statement
}
Example:
marks <- 75
if (marks >= 40) {
print("Pass")
}
Output: "Pass"
If:
marks <- 30
nothing is printed because the condition is FALSE.

if Statement with Multiple Commands: An if block can contain multiple statements.
marks <- 85

if (marks >= 80) {
print("Excellent")
print("Grade A+")
print("High performance")
}
Output:
[1] "Excellent"
[1] "Grade A+"
[1] "High performance"

if-else Statement: The if-else statement is used when there are two possible outcomes.
Syntax:
if (condition) {
statement_if_true
} else {
statement_if_false
}
Example:
marks <- 35
if (marks >= 40) {
print("Pass")
} else {
print("Fail")
}
Output: "Fail"
Practical Example: Voting Eligibility
age <- 19
if (age >= 18) {
print("Eligible to vote")
} else {
print("Not eligible to vote")

}
Output: "Eligible to vote"
Practical Example: Even or Odd: The %% operator returns the remainder after division.
number <- 15
if (number %% 2 == 0) {
print("Even")
} else {
print("Odd")
}
Since: 15 %% 2 = 1, the number is odd
else if Statement: If there are more than two possible outcomes, use else if.
Syntax:
if (condition1) {
} else if (condition2) {
} else if (condition3) {
} else {}

R code:
marks <- 76
if (marks >= 80) {
grade <- "A+"
} else if (marks >= 70) {
grade <- "A"
} else if (marks >= 60) {

grade <- "B"
} else if (marks >= 50) {
grade <- "C"
} else if (marks >= 40) {
grade <- "D"
} else {
grade <- "F"
}
print(grade)
Output: "A"
Nested Conditions: A nested condition means placing one conditional statement inside
another.
General structure:
if (condition1) {
if (condition2) {
statement
}
}
The inner condition is evaluated only when the outer condition is TRUE.
Example: Nested Conditions
age <- 25
gender <- "Female"
if (age >= 18) {
if (gender == "Female") {
