Looping in R
Looping is a programming technique used to execute a block of code repeatedly. Instead
of writing the same statements multiple times, we can use a loop to repeat them
automatically.
For example, suppose we want to print the numbers 1 to 5. Without a loop:
print(1)
print(2)
print(3)
print(4)
print(5)
Using a loop:
for (i in 1:5) {
print(i)
}

For Loop in R
A for loop is used when we know the sequence or number of times that we want to execute
a block of statements.
Basic Syntax
for (variable in sequence) {
statements
}
Components
• variable → takes one value at a time from the sequence
• sequence → values through which the loop moves

• statements → code executed during each iteration
Example
for (i in 1:5) {
print(i)
}

Print numbers from 1 to 10:
for (i in 1:10) {
print(i)
}
Print even numbers:
for (i in 1:20) {
if (i %% 2 == 0) {
print(i)
}
}
Print odd numbers:
for (i in 1:20) {
if (i %% 2 != 0) {
print(i)
}
}
For Loop Through a Vector:
A for loop can directly iterate through the elements of a vector.
students <- c("Rahim", "Karim", "Sumaiya", "Nusrat")
for (name in students) {
print(name)
}
Here, name takes each element of the vector.

For Loop with a Character Vector:
subjects <- c("Statistics", "R", "Python", "Database")
for (subject in subjects) {
print(subject)
}
For Loop with a Sequence
The seq() function can be useful for creating sequences.
for (i in seq(2, 10, by = 2)) {
print(i)
}
For Loop for Calculations
Suppose we want to calculate the square of numbers from 1 to 5.
for (i in 1:5) {
square <- i^2
print(square)
}
Calculate Sum Using a For Loop
Suppose:
x <- c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)
We can calculate the sum manually using a loop.
total <- 0
for (i in x) {
total <- total + i
}
print(total)
Calculate Mean Using a For Loop
x <- c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)
total <- 0
for (i in x) {
total <- total + i
}

mean_value <- total / length(x)
print(mean_value)

For Loop with an Index
Sometimes we need to know the position of each element.
x <- c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)
for (i in 1:length(x)) {
print(x[i])
}
For Loop and Conditional Statements
Loops can be combined with if, if-else, and nested conditions.
For example, classify students according to marks.
marks <- c(85, 72, 48, 91, 65)
for (mark in marks) {
if (mark >= 80) {
print("A")
} else if (mark >= 60) {
print("B")
} else {
print("C")
}
}
Nested For Loop
A nested loop is a loop inside another loop.
Example
for (i in 1:3) {
for (j in 1:3) {
print(paste(i, j))
}
}

The outer loop executes three times, and for each iteration, the inner loop executes three
times.
Creating a Multiplication Table
A nested loop can be used to create multiplication tables.
for (i in 1:5) {
for (j in 1:10) {
print(paste(i, "×", j, "=", i * j))
}
}
Statistical Application
Suppose the marks of five students are:
marks <- c(55, 67, 72, 81, 90)
We want to identify whether each student has passed.
for (mark in marks) {
if (mark >= 40) {
print("Pass")
} else {
print("Fail")
}
}
Count Number of Even Values
x <- c(12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27)
count <- 0
for (i in x) {
if (i %% 2 == 0) {
count <- count + 1
}
}
print(count)

While Loop in R
A while loop repeatedly executes statements as long as a specified condition remains
TRUE.
Syntax
while (condition) {
statements
}
The condition is checked before every iteration.
Print 1 to 5
i <- 1
while (i <= 5) {
print(i)
i <- i + 1
}
While Loop for Even Numbers
i <- 2
while (i <= 20) {
print(i)
i <- i + 2
}
While Loop for Calculating a Sum
i <- 1
total <- 0
while (i <= 10) {
total <- total + i
i <- i + 1
}
print(total)

Example: Suppose we want to determine how many numbers from 1 onward are required
for the cumulative sum to exceed 100.
i <- 1
total <- 0
while (total <= 100) {
total <- total + i
i <- i + 1
}
print(i - 1)
print(total)

Repeat Loop in R
The repeat loop executes a block of code again and again without automatically
checking a stopping condition.
Syntax
repeat {statements if (condition) {
break
}
}
The break statement is normally required to terminate a repeat loop
Example
i <- 1
repeat {
print(i)
i <- i + 1
if (i > 5) {
break
}
}

Repeat Loop for Sum
i <- 1
total <- 0
repeat {
total <- total + i
i <- i + 1
if (i > 10) {
break
}
}
print(total)

break Statement
The break statement immediately terminates a loop.
Example
for (i in 1:10) {
if (i == 6) {
break
}
print(i)
}
next Statement
The next statement skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.
Example
for (i in 1:10) {
if (i == 5) {
next
}
print(i)
}

Apply Family Functions in R
The apply family is a group of R functions designed to perform the same operation
repeatedly over elements of a vector, list, matrix, array, or grouped data.

apply(): apply() is used to apply a function over the rows or columns of a matrix or
array.
Basic syntax: apply(X, MARGIN, FUN, ...)
where:
• X = matrix or array
• MARGIN = dimension over which the function is applied
• FUN = function to apply
• ... = additional arguments to the function
For a matrix:
MARGIN = 1 → rows
MARGIN = 2 → columns
Matrix
x <- matrix(1:12, nrow = 3)
x
Row sums: apply(x, 1, sum)
Column sums: apply(x, 2, sum)
Row means: apply(x, 1, mean)
Column means: apply(x, 2, mean

We can use functions such as:
apply(x, 1, min)
apply(x, 1, max)
apply(x, 1, median)
apply(x, 1, sd)

lapply()
• lapply() means list apply.
• It applies a function to each element of a list or vector and always returns a list.
Syntax: lapply(X, FUN, ...)
Example 1
x <- list(
a = 1:5,
b = 10:15,
c = 20:25
)
lapply(x, mean)

Square Numbers
x <- list(
a = 1:3,
b = 4:6,
c = 7:9
)
lapply(x, function(z) z^2)
lapply() with Data Frames: A data frame is essentially a list of columns.
Suppose:
data <- data.frame(
age = c(20, 25, 30, 35),
income = c(20000, 25000, 30000, 40000),
score = c(70, 80, 75, 90))

Calculate the mean of every numeric column:
lapply(data, mean)
tapply(): tapply() is used to apply a function to a variable according to groups.
Syntax: tapply(X, INDEX, FUN, ...)
where:
• X = data vector
• INDEX = grouping variable/factor
• FUN = function to apply
Calculate a statistic separately for each group.
Simple tapply() Example
Suppose:
marks <- c(70, 80, 65, 90, 75, 85)
group <- c(
"A", "A", "B", "B", "A", "B"
)
Calculate the mean marks for each group:
tapply(marks, group, mean)

tapply() for Gender Groups
Suppose:
age <- c(20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45)
gender <- c(
"Male", "Female", "Male",
"Female", "Male", "Female"
)
Calculate mean age by gender:
tapply(age, gender, mean)

tapply() with More Than One Grouping Variable
tapply() can use multiple grouping variables.
For example:
age <- c(20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45)
sex <- c(
"Male", "Female", "Male",
"Female", "Male", "Female"
)
region <- c(
"Urban", "Urban", "Rural",
"Rural", "Urban", "Rural"
)
tapply(age, list(sex, region), mean)

Data Visualization

Scatter plots

• In a graph in which the values of two variables are plotted along the X-axis and Y-
axis, the pattern of the resulting points reveals a correlation between them.

Syntax: plot(x, y, main, xlab, ylab, xlim, ylim, axes)
Parameters:
• x: Sets the horizontal coordinates.
• y: Sets the vertical coordinates.
• xlab: Label for the horizontal axis.
• ylab: Label for the vertical axis.
• main: Title of the chart.
• xlim: Defines the x-axis range.
• ylim: Defines the y-axis range
• axes: Indicates whether both axes should be drawn.

R code:
x <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
y <- c(2, 5, 4, 8, 10)
plot(x, y,
main = "Scatter Plot",
xlab = "X values",
ylab = "Y values",
pch = 19)
If your data are in a data frame:
df <- data.frame(
height = c(150, 160, 165, 170, 180),
weight = c(50, 58, 62, 70, 80)
)
plot(df$height, df$weight,
main = "Height vs Weight", xlab = "Height",
ylab = "Weight",col = "blue", pch = 19)
Use ggplot2:
library(ggplot2)
ggplot(df, aes(x = height, y = weight)) +
geom_point() +
labs(
title = "Height vs Weight",
x = "Height",
y = "Weight"
) +
theme_minimal()
Different colors based on a group

x <- 1:10
y <- c(3, 5, 4, 7, 8, 10, 9, 12, 11, 14)
group <- c("A", "A", "A", "A", "A",
"B", "B", "B", "B", "B")
plot(x, y, pch = 19,
col = ifelse(group == "A", "blue", "red"),
main = "Scatter Plot by Group",
xlab = "X",
ylab = "Y")

legend("topleft",
legend = c("Group A", "Group B"),
col = c("blue", "red"),
